# Spectral Threshold Foundation for the Discrete Hodge Program

This file is the **decomposition / spectral foundation** on which the message-passing
cycles (`HodgeMessagePassingConvergence`, `HodgeMessagePassingDuality`,
`HodgeMessagePassingEnergy`, `HodgeDeepLimit`) are built.

The abstract combinatorial Hodge Laplacian is the sum of an *up* (coboundary∘boundary)
and a *down* (boundary∘coboundary) piece,

  `Δ = up + down`  (`hodgeLaplacian up down`),

each of which is symmetric and positive semidefinite (PSD) for the inner product.
The headline structural fact of the cycle is the **harmonic characterisation**

  `Δ x = 0  ↔  up x = 0 ∧ down x = 0`   (`harmonic_iff`),

i.e. a cochain is harmonic (= a representative of Hodge cohomology) exactly when it is
simultaneously *closed* (`down x = 0`) and *coclosed* (`up x = 0`).  The non-trivial
direction rests on the Cauchy–Schwarz fact that a symmetric PSD operator `A` has
`⟪A x, x⟫ = 0 → A x = 0` (`psd_inner_self_eq_zero`); applied to `up` and `down`
separately after splitting the Dirichlet energy `⟪Δx,x⟫ = ⟪up x,x⟫ + ⟪down x,x⟫`.
As a submodule statement this reads `ker Δ = ker up ⊓ ker down`
(`ker_hodgeLaplacian`).

-- !-- Lab Notebook -- !--
Hypothesis:  The Hodge Laplacian `Δ = up + down` is symmetric PSD, and its kernel is
  the intersection of the kernels of the two PSD pieces — because the Dirichlet energy
  splits as a sum of two nonnegative terms, each of which must vanish on a harmonic
  cochain, and a symmetric PSD operator is determined to be zero by the vanishing of
  its diagonal quadratic form.
Result:  Formalised and proved sorry-free.  `psd_inner_self_eq_zero` (the Cauchy–Schwarz
  kernel criterion), `hodgeLaplacian_symm` / `hodgeLaplacian_pos` (Δ is symmetric PSD),
  `hodgeLaplacian_energy_split` (additive Dirichlet energy), `harmonic_iff`
  (closed-and-coclosed characterisation), and `ker_hodgeLaplacian` (submodule form).
Insight:  The decisive lemma is `psd_inner_self_eq_zero`: for a symmetric PSD `A`,
  the form `B(u,v) = ⟪A u, v⟫` is a semidefinite symmetric bilinear form, so the
  Cauchy–Schwarz inequality `B(x,y)² ≤ B(x,x)·B(y,y)` forces `B(x, A x) = ‖A x‖² = 0`
  once `B(x,x) = 0`.  No spectral theorem or finite-dimensionality is needed.
Failure analysis:  Working with the inner-product energy `⟪A x, x⟫` (rather than the
  norm) keeps every estimate polynomial and avoids `Real.sqrt`; phrasing the quadratic
  `0 ≤ ⟪A (x - t • A x), x - t • A x⟫` in `t` lets a single `nlinarith`/derivative-free
  argument extract `‖A x‖² = 0`.
-- !-- Lab Notebook -- !--
